DTEND:20260814T003000Z
DESCRIPTION:GW Los Angeles Reception with Elliott School Dean Alyssa AyresD
 ate and TimeThursday‚ August 13\n6:30 pm - 8:30 pm\n\nLocationKimpton Ho
 tel Palomar Beverly Hills\n10740 Wilshire Blvd\nLos Angeles‚ CA 90024\n\
 nEvent DescriptionThe GW community in Los Angeles is invited to join us fo
 r a reception and discussion with Elliott School Dean Alyssa Ayres. Our al
 umni panelists will discuss their varied careers in international affairs.
  \n\nRegistration for the event is $15 for alumni‚ parents‚ and guests
 ‚ and includes hors d’oeuvres‚ beer‚ wine‚ and soft drinks. Stud
 ents are free.\n\nSpeakersDr. Alexis Blanc‚ ESIA MA ’08‚ CCAS MA ’
 16‚ PhD ’19‚ is a Senior Political Scientist at RAND. Dr. Blanc spec
 ializes in a diverse set of national security issues: nuclear deterrence d
 ynamics and strategic stability‚ nuclear force posture‚ long-range str
 ike‚ and crisis stability and escalation management strategies. She also
  has expertise in the space domain‚ examining many of these same issues 
 as well as mission assurance for nuclear command and control and supply ch
 ain security. Her recent work has focused on the use of non-dedicated syst
 ems for nuclear command and control‚ nuclear design certification‚ and
  force structure to confront adversary nuclear saber rattling. Prior to RA
 ND‚ she was a senior federal program manager at the Department of Energy
 ’s National Nuclear Security Administration for nine years‚ managing c
 onstruction projects for the Office of Advanced Simulation and Computing. 
 She completed multiple special projects for the NNSA Administrator’s Pol
 icy Office‚ working on nuclear weapons policy‚ posture‚ and supply c
 hain security. \n\n \n\nStephen Ryan‚ ESIA BA ’07‚ is Director‚ Ai
 r-Space Integration at Northrop Grumman Space Systems. He is a senior tech
 nical leader and manager bridging advanced missions‚ strategy‚ enginee
 ring‚ and technology to execute next-generation space system development
  and production programs focused on air-space integration and support to a
 dvanced joint warfighting concepts. Prior to joining Northrop Grumman‚ R
 yan was a decorated intelligence analyst with the National Geospatial-Inte
 lligence Agency (NGA)‚ where he specialized in terrorism analysis focuse
 d on Afghanistan and Pakistan. At NGA‚ he deployed to Afghanistan multip
 le times in support of U.S. special operations forces. Ryan’s awards inc
 lude the Joint Civilian Service Commendation Award‚ the NATO Medal‚ th
 e National Intelligence Meritorious Unit Citation‚ and the National Inte
 lligence Professionals Award for Intelligence Integration. Ryan is a membe
 r of the Pacific Council on International Policy‚ a senior member of the
  American Institute of Aeronautics and Astronautics‚ and was a term memb
 er at the Council on Foreign Relations.\n\n \n\nTracy Williams‚ ESIA BA 
 ’83‚ is the founder of Olmstead Williams Communications‚ a Los Angel
 es based public relations agency specializing in reputation management and
  crisis communication for companies in tech‚ healthcare and financial se
 rvices. The firm employs social and digital strategies‚ including AI‚ 
 along with traditional public relations capabilities. The firm has earned 
 more than 20 industry awards for its work with leading companies including
  Johnson & Johnson and nonprofit leaders including Didi Hirsch Mental Heal
 th Services. Williams was named a Top Woman in PR by PRNews‚ and the age
 ncy was a finalist for the Agency Elite Awards. With a career start at Hil
 l and Knowlton‚ Inc. in Washington‚ D.C.‚ Williams guided U.S. and E
 uropean defense manufacturers through the government acquisition process. 
 She developed programs aimed at key decision-makers in the U.S. Congress
 ‚ the Department of Defense‚ the Department of State‚ and other fede
 ral agencies. She served as co-chair on the Los Angeles World Affairs Coun
 cil and is a board member and judge for the Women Founder’s Network. \n\
 n \n\n\n\nModerator: Appointed in February 2021‚ Alyssa Ayres is the fir
 st woman to serve as permanent dean at the Elliott School. She is a foreig
 n policy practitioner and award-winning author with senior experience in t
 he government‚ nonprofit‚ and private sectors. From 2013 to 2021‚ sh
 e was a senior fellow for India‚ Pakistan‚ and South Asia at the Counc
 il on Foreign Relations‚ where she remains an adjunct senior fellow‚ a
 nd from 2010 to 2013 served as deputy assistant secretary of state for Sou
 th Asia. Her work focuses primarily on India’s role in the world and on 
 U.S. relations with South Asia in the larger Indo-Pacific. Ayres received 
 an AB from Harvard College and an MA and PhD from the University of Chicag
 o. She is a member of the Council on Foreign Relations‚ the Trilateral C
 ommission‚ the Halifax International Security Forum’s agenda group‚ 
 and serves on the board of directors of the National Endowment for Democra
 cy and the Women’s Foreign Policy Group. In 2021 and 2022‚ the Washing
 tonian included her as one of their “most influential people shaping pol
 icy.”\n\nOnline registration is now closed. However‚ you may still att
 end. Please contact Elaine Garbe in Elliott School Alumni Programs at egar
 be@gwu.edu for more information. We hope to see you there!
GEO:34.054908;-118.242643
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:GW Los Angeles Reception with Elliott School Dean Alyssa Ayres (Los
  Angeles&sbquo\; CA)
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/gw-los-angeles-reception-with-
 elliott-school-dean-alyssa-ayres-los-angelessbquo-ca
CATEGORIES:Alumni
END:VEVENT
