DESCRIPTION:On September 24\, Chinese President and Communist Party General
  Secretary Xi Jinping will visit the White House for the first state visit
  to the United States by the Chinese leader since 2015. In the interim the
  US-China relationship has taken a tumultuous course primarily characteriz
 ed by multiple frictions and comprehensive competition. More recently\, in
  the wake of President Trump's state visit to China in May (which Xi's vis
 it is reciprocating) there seems to be some new stability and lowered tens
 ions in the relationship--but to what extent and can it endure? In this le
 cture Professor David Shambaugh\, Director of the China Policy Program at 
 GWU\, will consider the evolution of the relationship over the past decade
 \, its current state\, and possible future trajectories.\n\nDoors will ope
 n no sooner than 15 minutes before the start time.\n\nThe Elliott School c
 an coordinate with the university to reasonably accommodate most disabilit
 ies. If you need specific accommodations\, please contact the Sigur Center
  at gsigur@gwu.edu. Requests should be made as soon as possible\, but at l
 east three days prior to the program to ensure accommodation.
GEO:38.896188;-77.044206
LOCATION:1957 E Street NW\, State Room
SUMMARY:America <-> Asia: Another Sino-American Summit: What Has & Hasn’t
  Changed in the US-China Relationship?
