DESCRIPTION:At a time of growing geopolitical fragmentation and global unce
 rtainty\, development cooperation has become increasingly important to sus
 taining an open\, rules-based international order and addressing shared ch
 allenges. Drawing on Japan’s experience\, JICA President Akihiko Tanaka 
 will discuss how partnerships with the Global South can promote human secu
 rity\, economic opportunity\, technological innovation\, and a more inclus
 ive international order. He will also explore how development cooperation\
 , rather than simply providing assistance or charity\, can serve as a long
 -term investment in managing global risks and advancing the security and p
 rosperity of both developing and advanced countries.\n\nThis talk is broug
 ht to you by the Sigur Center for Asian Studies\, in conjunction with the 
 International Development Studies Program and the Institute for Internatio
 nal Economic Policy.\n\nDoors will open no sooner than 15 minutes before t
 he start time.\n\nThe Elliott School can coordinate with the university to
  reasonably accommodate most disabilities. If you need specific accommodat
 ions\, please contact the Sigur Center at gsigur@gwu.edu. Requests should 
 be made as soon as possible\, but at least three days prior to the program
  to ensure accommodation.
GEO:38.896188;-77.044206
LOCATION:1957 E Street NW\, Lindner Family Commons
SUMMARY:Development Cooperation in a Fragmented World: A Japanese Perspecti
 ve
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/development-cooperation-in-a-f
 ragmented-world-a-japanese-perspective
